---
// Reader-facing audit surface, same contract as the dip piece's: the rule
// stated precisely enough to re-implement, the exact figures to check against,
// and a script that downloads both datasets and re-derives everything from
// scratch. The script IS our pipeline for this piece — not a re-implementation
// of it — and it printed ALL FIGURES REPRODUCED from a clean download before
// this shipped.
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';

const CHECKS = [
  { split: 'Yield higher than a year earlier', n: '434', med: '+11.9%', pos: '73.7%' },
  { split: 'Yield lower than a year earlier', n: '381', med: '+13.6%', pos: '80.3%' },
  { split: 'Rising yields, inflation under 4% (real)', n: '263', med: '+8.7%', pos: '74.5%' },
  { split: 'Rising yields, inflation 4%+ (real)', n: '171', med: '+4.4%', pos: '56.1%' },
];
---

<div class="rc-a">
  <div class="rc-a-head">
    <span class="rc-a-lbl">Check it yourself</span>
    <span class="rc-a-sub">Everything here is free, public and re-runnable</span>
  </div>

  <div class="rc-a-body">
    <p class="rc-a-lead">
      We would rather you did not take our word for it. The rule takes an afternoon to
      re-implement, both datasets are free, and the figures you should get are printed
      below.
    </p>

    <p class="rc-a-h">The rule, stated exactly</p>
    <ol class="rc-a-steps">
      <li>Take every month from April 1954 that has a full year of data behind it and a
        full year of share returns ahead of it &mdash; 819 of them, through June 2022.</li>
      <li>Behind it, measure two things: did the 10-year Treasury yield <b>rise or fall</b> over
        the trailing 12 months (FRED&rsquo;s monthly averages), and what was CPI
        inflation over those same 12 months?</li>
      <li>Ahead of it, measure the next 12 months of US share <b>total return</b>,
        dividends reinvested &mdash; once as printed, once after inflation.</li>
      <li>Sort the months by what was behind them, and compare what came next.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="rc-a-warn">
      Use total return, not the bare index. Dividends were a third of the sixties&rsquo;
      story, and leaving them out is how the same history gets retold as a disaster.
    </p>

    <p class="rc-a-h">What you should get</p>
    <div class="rc-a-tw">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Sorted by</th><th>Months</th><th>Median next 12m</th><th>Positive</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {CHECKS.map((c) => (
            <tr><td>{c.split}</td><td>{c.n}</td><td>{c.med}</td><td>{c.pos}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="rc-a-note">
      First two rows are nominal; the inflation rows are real. Calendar-year check: the
      10-year rose at least half a point Dec&ndash;Dec in <b>21</b> years; the S&amp;P 500&rsquo;s
      total return was positive in <b>15</b>, and the losers were 1969, 1974, 1977, 1981,
      1987 and 2022. Small differences are expected if you handle months with no dividend
      figure differently from us.
    </p>

    <p class="rc-a-h">Or run ours</p>
    <p class="rc-a-lead">
      <a href="/data/rising-rates.py" download>Download the script</a> &mdash; one file of
      Python, written to be read. It fetches both datasets itself, recomputes every figure
      above plus the episodes and the 2&times;2, and prints <b>OK</b> or <b>MISMATCH</b> against
      what we published. If you get a mismatch, we would genuinely like to hear
      about it.
    </p>
    <pre class="rc-a-code"><code>pip install pandas numpy requests xlrd
python rising-rates.py</code></pre>

    <p class="rc-a-h">The data</p>
    <ul class="rc-a-src">
      <li><a href="http://www.econ.yale.edu/~shiller/data/ie_data.xls">Shiller, US prices, dividends and CPI from 1871</a> (Yale) &mdash; the <code>Data</code> sheet</li>
      <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/GS10">FRED series GS10</a> (St.&nbsp;Louis Fed) &mdash; monthly-average 10-year constant maturity yield from April 1953</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="rc-a-foot"><ToolBrand /></div>
</div>

<style is:global>
/* The audit surface ships folded: it is the trust signature, not the read.
   The one-line summary keeps the promise visible; opening it is opt-in. */
.post-content details.rc-fold{margin:26px 0;border:1px solid var(--color-border);
  border-radius:12px;background:#fff;
  box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.05),0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.04)}
.post-content .rc-fold>summary{cursor:pointer;padding:14px 20px;font-size:14.5px;
  font-weight:700;color:var(--color-dark);background:var(--color-surface);
  border-radius:12px;list-style-position:inside}
.post-content .rc-fold>summary::marker{color:var(--color-brand-700)}
